<template>
  <div class="rag-story">
    <ProjectPageHeader project-id="pwcRag" context="PwC" data-scroll-reveal>
      <h1>A Structure-Preserving RAG Ingestion Pipeline</h1>
      <p class="project-subtitle">Keeping rules and their conditions together when preparing PDFs for an AI assistant</p>
    </ProjectPageHeader>

    <article class="project-narrative" data-scroll-group data-scroll-step="110">
      <p class="lede">
        At PwC, I worked on the document pipeline behind a public-enquiry assistant. When someone asked a question,
        the system used retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) to find relevant passages from published rules and place
        them in front of a language model before it wrote an answer. The answer could only be as complete as the
        evidence that arrived with the question.
      </p>
      <p>
        The difficult cases did not necessarily look broken. A sentence could state a rule clearly, then point to a
        note four pages later that limited when the rule applied. A table could continue across a page break. A
        flowchart could contain every relevant word, while the arrows between those words carried the actual decision.
        A parser might recover all the visible content and still lose the relationship that changed its meaning.
      </p>
      <p>
        This shifted my attention away from extraction accuracy alone. The knowledge-base ingestion pipeline had to
        repair the connections a careful human reader would follow across the page before the documents were divided
        into searchable chunks. Then, when the assistant retrieved a rule, its qualifications could travel with it.
      </p>

      <h2 class="project-chapter-first">Why one PDF needed several ways of reading</h2>
      <p>
        I started by looking at what each kind of document element asked a reader to understand. Paragraphs and tables
        mostly depended on order and layout. A reference such as Note (4) depended on finding the matching note
        elsewhere in the section. A flowchart depended on seeing which shape led to which branch. Treating all three
        as the same extraction problem would have hidden those differences.
      </p>
      <p>
        For ordinary text and tables, I began with MinerU. It is a PDF parser that turns the visual layout of a page
        into structured text while keeping features such as rows, columns, and reading order. It handled much of the
        document well, which left a smaller and more meaningful set of failures to repair.
      </p>
      <p>
        The cross-page notes were explicit enough to repair with rules. When a sentence ended in (4), a linker could
        search the relevant section for Note (4), bring the two pieces together, and leave a trail that was easy to
        inspect. Flowcharts needed a different kind of reading. I used a vision-language model (VLM) to follow their
        shapes and arrows, then record the reconstructed diagram as Mermaid, a text format that preserves nodes and
        connections. Both results could then enter RAGFlow, the retrieval platform used in the proof of concept.
      </p>
      <p>
        It would have been simpler on paper to send every page through the VLM. In practice, that choice would increase
        cost, make more of the pipeline probabilistic, and leave the client with more model-dependent behaviour to
        maintain. I reserved visual reasoning for the places where layout carried meaning that deterministic methods
        could not recover. The rest stayed on a path whose output was easier to verify.
      </p>
      <p>
        I brought these paths together in a five-page test document, then followed the repaired relationships through
        chunking, retrieval, and the final answer. The stages below let you open that path one step at a time. The test
        results, the client’s existing infrastructure, and the ongoing operating cost all informed the recommendation.
      </p>

      <div class="stage-list" data-scroll-group data-scroll-step="120">
        <div
